// Computer Networks, Unit V: the application layer.
// Follows CN - Notes3 (Unit V, Forouzan-style) and Tanenbaum chapter 7.
export default {
  'cn.u5.dns': {
    oneLine: 'DNS, the Domain Name System, is a hierarchical, distributed database that maps human-friendly host names such as www.cbit.ac.in to IP addresses (and other records), so people never have to remember numbers.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'DNS (Domain Name System)', text: 'A **hierarchical, domain-based naming scheme** and a **distributed database system** for implementing it. It is used mainly to map **host names to IP addresses**, and also e-mail destinations to mail servers. Defined in RFCs 1034 and 1035.' },
      { h: 'Why DNS is needed' },
      'Programs could refer to hosts by IP address, but addresses are hard for people to remember, and if a server moves to a new address everyone must be told. ASCII names decouple machine names from addresses. In the ARPANET, a single file **hosts.txt** listed every host and was fetched nightly. For millions of hosts that cannot work: the file would be too large, and a single central server would be a bottleneck and a single point of failure, and name conflicts would occur constantly. The solution is to **distribute the information among many computers**; the host that needs a mapping contacts the closest computer that holds it.',
      { h: 'How DNS is used' },
      { steps: [
        'An application wants to reach a host by name, e.g. a file transfer client to afilesource.com.',
        'It calls a library procedure, the **resolver**, passing the name.',
        'The resolver sends a **query** containing the name to a **local DNS server**, using **UDP on port 53**.',
        'The DNS server looks up the name (asking other servers if necessary) and returns a **response** with the IP address.',
        'The resolver returns the address to the application, which can now open a TCP connection or send UDP packets to that host.',
      ] },
      { img: 'dns-resolution' },
      { h: 'The three parts of DNS' },
      { table: { head: ['Part', 'What it is', 'Topic'], rows: [['**Name space**', 'The hierarchical tree of domain names', 'The DNS Name Space'], ['**Resource records**', 'The data stored for each name (IP address, mail server, …)', 'Domain Resource Records'], ['**Name servers**', 'The distributed servers holding the zones and answering queries', 'Name Servers']] } },
      { h: 'Features' },
      { ul: ['**Hierarchical**: names are organised as an inverted tree.', '**Distributed**: no single machine holds all the data; each organisation manages its own part.', '**Replicated**: each zone is held by a primary and secondary servers, so it survives failures.', '**Cached**: servers remember answers for their time to live, so popular names are resolved quickly.', 'Uses **UDP port 53** for queries (TCP for large responses and zone transfers).'] },
      { analogy: 'DNS is the Internet\'s phone directory: you look up a name and get the number. Instead of one giant book, each city (domain) keeps its own book, and an index tells you which city\'s book to look in.' },
    ],
    terms: [['Resolver', 'The client library that sends DNS queries for applications.'], ['hosts.txt', 'The single file that mapped names to addresses before DNS.'], ['Port 53', 'The DNS port, mostly over UDP.']],
    exam: 'Define DNS, explain why a single hosts file does not scale, describe the resolution steps (application → resolver → local server → response), draw the recursive and iterative lookup, and name the three parts of DNS.',
    revise: ['Maps names ↔ IP addresses; distributed, hierarchical', 'Resolver → local DNS server over UDP 53', 'Name space, resource records, name servers'],
  },

  'cn.u5.dns-namespace': {
    oneLine: 'The DNS name space is an inverted tree of labels with a nameless root; a node\'s full domain name is its labels read up to the root, top-level domains are generic (com, edu, org) or country codes (in, uk), and a domain is any subtree.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'Name space', text: 'The set of all names. A name space that maps each address to a unique name can be **flat** (a name is a structureless sequence of characters, which needs central control and cannot scale) or **hierarchical** (each name is made of several parts, so the authority to assign names can be decentralised).' },
      { def: 'Domain name space', text: 'DNS\'s hierarchical name space, defined as an **inverted tree** with the **root at the top**. The tree can have only 128 levels (level 0, the root, to level 127).' },
      { img: 'cn-p96b' },
      { h: 'Labels and domain names' },
      { ul: [
        'Each node has a **label**, a string of at most **63 characters**. The root label is a **null (empty) string**.',
        'Children of the same node must have **different labels**, which guarantees unique domain names.',
        'Each node has a **domain name**: the sequence of labels from the node **up to the root**, separated by dots. A full name may be at most 255 characters.',
        'Domain names are **case-insensitive** (edu, Edu and EDU mean the same).',
      ] },
      { img: 'cn-p96a' },
      { compare: [
        { title: 'Fully qualified domain name (FQDN)', items: ['A label sequence ending in the **null root label**, so it ends with a dot', 'Contains the full name of a host from the node to the root', 'Example: **challenger.atc.fhda.edu.**', 'A DNS server can resolve it directly'] },
        { title: 'Partially qualified domain name (PQDN)', items: ['Does **not** end at the root: no trailing dot', 'Starts from a node but does not reach the root', 'Used when the name and the client are in the same site; the resolver adds a **suffix** to complete it', 'Example: **challenger** (completed as challenger.atc.fhda.edu.)'] },
      ] },
      { h: 'Domains' },
      { def: 'Domain', text: 'A **subtree** of the domain name space. The name of the domain is the name of the node at the top of the subtree. A domain may itself be divided into **subdomains**.' },
      { h: 'Top-level domains' },
      'The Internet is divided into over 250 top-level domains, each covering many hosts and partitioned into subdomains. Top-level domains are run by **registrars** appointed by ICANN.',
      { table: { head: ['Kind', 'Examples'], rows: [['**Generic** (gTLD)', 'com (commercial), edu (educational), gov (US government), int (international organisations), mil (US military), net (network providers), org (non-profits), plus aero, biz, coop, info, museum, name, pro, …'], ['**Country** (ccTLD)', 'One per country (ISO 3166): in (India), uk, jp, de, us, …'], ['**Inverse domain**', 'in-addr.arpa: used to map an IP address back to a name (reverse lookup)']] } },
      { example: 'In **eng.cbit.ac.in**: "in" is the country top-level domain, "ac" the academic subdomain of India, "cbit" the college, and "eng" a department. Reading from the left gives the host or department; reading to the right goes up to the root.' },
      'Naming follows **organisational boundaries**, not physical networks: two departments in the same building can be in different domains, and one domain can span many networks. To create a new subdomain, permission is needed only from the domain that will contain it.',
    ],
    terms: [['Label', 'The name of one node in the tree; up to 63 characters.'], ['FQDN', 'A complete domain name ending at the root (trailing dot).'], ['PQDN', 'A partial name completed by the resolver with a suffix.'], ['TLD', 'Top-level domain: generic or country code.']],
    exam: 'Compare flat and hierarchical name spaces, draw the inverted tree with the root, TLDs and subdomains, define label, domain name and domain, explain FQDN vs PQDN with examples (asked in Nov 2025), and list generic and country domains.',
    revise: ['Inverted tree, root = null label, 128 levels', 'Label ≤ 63 chars; name read up to the root', 'FQDN ends with a dot; PQDN does not', 'Generic: com, edu, org …; country: in, uk …'],
  },

  'cn.u5.dns-rr': {
    oneLine: 'Every domain has a set of resource records, each a five-tuple (domain name, time to live, class, type, value); the type says what the value is, e.g. A for an IPv4 address, MX for a mail server, NS for a name server, CNAME for an alias.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'Resource record (RR)', text: 'An entry in the DNS database associated with a domain. When a resolver gives a domain name to DNS, what it gets back are the **resource records** for that name. The primary function of DNS is to map domain names onto resource records.' },
      { h: 'Format: a five-tuple' },
      { code: 'Domain_name    Time_to_live    Class    Type    Value\ncbit.ac.in.    86400           IN       MX      1 mail.cbit.ac.in.' },
      { table: { head: ['Field', 'Meaning'], rows: [
        ['**Domain_name**', 'The domain this record applies to; it is the primary search key. Many records usually exist per domain.'],
        ['**Time_to_live**', 'How stable the record is, in seconds: how long it may be **cached**. Stable information gets a large value (86400 = one day); volatile information a small one (60 = one minute).'],
        ['**Class**', 'For Internet information it is always **IN**. Other codes exist but are rarely seen.'],
        ['**Type**', 'What kind of record this is (see the table below).'],
        ['**Value**', 'A number, domain name or ASCII string, whose meaning depends on the type.'],
      ] } },
      { h: 'The principal record types' },
      { table: { head: ['Type', 'Meaning', 'Value'], rows: [
        ['**SOA**', 'Start of authority', 'Parameters for this zone: primary name server, administrator\'s e-mail, serial number, timeouts'],
        ['**A**', 'IPv4 address of a host', '32-bit integer'],
        ['**AAAA**', 'IPv6 address of a host', '128-bit integer'],
        ['**MX**', 'Mail exchange', 'Priority and the domain willing to accept e-mail (lower priority number preferred)'],
        ['**NS**', 'Name server', 'Name of a server for this domain'],
        ['**CNAME**', 'Canonical name', 'Domain name: an **alias** points to the real (canonical) name'],
        ['**PTR**', 'Pointer', 'Alias for an IP address: used for reverse lookup in in-addr.arpa'],
        ['**SPF**', 'Sender policy framework', 'Text encoding of the policy for sending e-mail (fights spoofing)'],
        ['**SRV**', 'Service', 'Host that provides a given service'],
        ['**TXT**', 'Text', 'Descriptive ASCII text'],
      ] } },
      { h: 'Example zone (abridged)' },
      { code: `; Authoritative data for cs.vu.nl
cs.vu.nl.          86400  IN  SOA    star boss (9527,7200,7200,241920,86400)
cs.vu.nl.          86400  IN  MX     1 zephyr
cs.vu.nl.          86400  IN  MX     2 top
cs.vu.nl.          86400  IN  NS     star
star               86400  IN  A      130.37.56.205
zephyr             86400  IN  A      130.37.20.10
top                86400  IN  A      130.37.20.11
www                86400  IN  CNAME  star.cs.vu.nl
ftp                86400  IN  CNAME  zephyr.cs.vu.nl` },
      'Reading it: mail for cs.vu.nl goes first to zephyr (priority 1) and, if that fails, to top (priority 2). www.cs.vu.nl and ftp.cs.vu.nl are aliases (CNAMEs) for the machines star and zephyr. The A records give their IP addresses.',
      { h: 'Glue records' },
      'A name server record (NS) for a subdomain names a server inside that same subdomain, so its A record must also be stored in the parent zone; otherwise the server could never be found. These extra A records are called **glue records**.',
    ],
    terms: [['TTL', 'How many seconds a record may be cached.'], ['A record', 'Maps a name to an IPv4 address.'], ['MX record', 'Names the mail server for a domain, with a priority.'], ['CNAME', 'An alias pointing to the canonical name.'], ['PTR', 'Maps an address back to a name.']],
    exam: 'Define a resource record, give the five-tuple format with the meaning of each field, tabulate the main types (SOA, A, AAAA, MX, NS, CNAME, PTR, TXT), and write a small example zone explaining each line.',
    revise: ['(Domain_name, TTL, Class IN, Type, Value)', 'A = IPv4, AAAA = IPv6, MX = mail server, NS = name server, CNAME = alias, PTR = reverse, SOA = zone info', 'TTL controls caching'],
  },

  'cn.u5.name-servers': {
    oneLine: 'The DNS name space is divided into non-overlapping zones, each served by a primary and secondary name servers; root servers sit at the top, and a query is resolved recursively between the host and its local server and iteratively from there, with caching at every step.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'Name server', text: 'A server that holds the resource records for part of the name space and answers queries about it. A single server holding the whole database would be overloaded and a single point of failure, so the name space is divided into **non-overlapping zones**, each with its own name servers.' },
      { def: 'Zone', text: 'The part of the domain tree a server is **responsible for** (has authority over). It equals the domain if the server has not delegated any subdomains; otherwise the delegated subdomains belong to other zones.' },
      { h: 'Kinds of name servers' },
      { table: { head: ['Server', 'Role'], rows: [
        ['**Root servers**', 'At the top of the hierarchy: they know the name servers for every top-level domain. There are 13 root server names (a.root-servers.net to m.root-servers.net), each replicated at many sites using anycast.'],
        ['**Primary (master) server**', 'Stores the **zone file** it is authoritative for, from a file on its disk; it creates, maintains and updates the records.'],
        ['**Secondary (slave) server**', 'Gets the zone information from the primary by a **zone transfer**; it holds an authoritative copy but does not create or update it. Provides redundancy and shares the load.'],
        ['**Local (caching) server**', 'The server a host\'s resolver asks first (at the ISP or campus). It resolves names on the host\'s behalf and **caches** results.'],
      ] } },
      'An answer that comes from the authority managing the record is **authoritative**; one from a cache is **non-authoritative** and may be out of date.',
      { h: 'Name resolution' },
      { img: 'dns-resolution' },
      { steps: [
        'The resolver on the host sends the query (e.g. for robot.cs.washington.edu) to its **local name server**. This query is **recursive**: the local server must return the complete answer.',
        'If the local server does not have the answer cached, it asks a **root server**. The root replies with a **referral**: the name servers for the .edu top-level domain.',
        'The local server asks the **TLD server**, which refers it to the name server for washington.edu.',
        'That server refers it to the name server for cs.washington.edu, which is **authoritative** and returns the A record.',
        'The local server returns the answer to the resolver and **caches** it (and the referrals) for their TTL.',
      ] },
      { compare: [
        { title: 'Recursive query', items: ['The server takes the query and returns the **final answer**', 'Puts the burden on the server', 'Used between a host and its local DNS server'] },
        { title: 'Iterative query', items: ['The server returns the answer or a **referral** to another server; the asker continues', 'Keeps root and TLD servers light', 'Used from the local server to root, TLD and authoritative servers'] },
      ] },
      { h: 'Caching' },
      'Every server caches the answers it gets. If another host asks for the same name, or for another name in the same domain, the answer (or at least the referral) comes from the cache, avoiding queries to the root. Cached records expire after their **TTL**, so out-of-date information does not live forever. Most queries never reach the root servers.',
      { h: 'Reverse lookup' },
      'To find the name for an IP address, DNS uses the **in-addr.arpa** domain: the address 130.37.56.205 is looked up as 205.56.37.130.in-addr.arpa, and the answer is a **PTR** record.',
    ],
    terms: [['Zone', 'The part of the name space a server is authoritative for.'], ['Zone transfer', 'Copying a zone from the primary to a secondary server.'], ['Referral', 'A reply naming another server to ask.'], ['Authoritative answer', 'An answer from the server that manages the record.']],
    exam: 'Define name server and zone, describe root, primary, secondary and local servers, draw and explain the resolution of a name step by step, contrast recursive and iterative queries, and explain caching with TTL.',
    revise: ['Zones are non-overlapping; primary + secondary per zone', 'Root → TLD → authoritative', 'Host ↔ local: recursive; local ↔ others: iterative', 'Caching with TTL; reverse lookup via in-addr.arpa'],
  },

  'cn.u5.email-arch': {
    oneLine: 'Internet e-mail is built from user agents (to compose and read mail), message transfer agents (mail servers that relay mail with SMTP) and message access agents (POP3 or IMAP to fetch it), with mailboxes and a spool queue on the servers.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'Electronic mail', text: 'A system that lets users exchange messages. Unlike HTTP or FTP it is a **one-way transaction**: Alice sends to Bob and may or may not get a reply. Because Bob cannot run a server all the time, **intermediate mail servers** hold the mail, and users run only client programs.' },
      { h: 'Architecture' },
      { img: 'cn-email' },
      'Alice and Bob are connected to two mail servers. The administrator has created one **mailbox** per user (a special file on the server\'s disk that only its owner may access) and a **queue (spool)** for messages waiting to be sent. Three kinds of agents are used:',
      { table: { head: ['Component', 'Role', 'Protocol'], rows: [
        ['**User agent (UA)**', 'Program the user runs to compose, read, reply to, forward and file messages', '-'],
        ['**Message transfer agent (MTA)**', 'Mail server software that moves mail from the sender\'s site to the receiver\'s: an MTA client pushes, an MTA server always runs and accepts', '**SMTP**'],
        ['**Message access agent (MAA)**', 'Lets the receiver pull mail from the mailbox on the server to their own computer', '**POP3** or **IMAP4**'],
      ] } },
      { h: 'The nine steps (Alice to Bob)' },
      { steps: [
        'Alice composes the message in her **UA**.',
        'Her UA sends it to her mail server with an **MTA client** (SMTP).',
        'Her mail server\'s **MTA server** puts it in the **spool** queue.',
        'The MTA client at her server takes the message from the queue …',
        '… and sends it across the Internet with **SMTP** to the **MTA server** at Bob\'s mail server.',
        'Bob\'s server stores it in **Bob\'s mailbox**.',
        'Later Bob runs his **MAA client** …',
        '… which connects to the **MAA server** (POP3 or IMAP) on his mail server …',
        '… and retrieves the message to his **UA**, where he reads it.',
      ] },
      'So e-mail needs **three uses of the client-server paradigm**: two SMTP **push** stages (Alice → her server, her server → Bob\'s server) and one **pull** stage (Bob\'s server → Bob) with POP3 or IMAP.',
      { h: 'Basic functions of an e-mail system (Tanenbaum)' },
      { ul: ['**Composition**: creating messages and answers.', '**Transfer**: moving messages from originator to recipient.', '**Reporting**: telling the originator what happened (delivered, rejected, lost).', '**Displaying** incoming messages (including multimedia).', '**Disposition**: what the recipient does with a message (throw away, save, forward).', 'Extra: mailing lists, CC/BCC, high-priority mail, secret (encrypted) mail, alternative recipients, mail forwarding, vacation daemons.'] },
      { h: 'E-mail addresses' },
      'An address has the form **local-part@domain-name**: the local part is the user\'s mailbox; the domain name is looked up in DNS (an **MX record**) to find the mail server that accepts mail for that domain.',
    ],
    terms: [['UA', 'User agent: the mail client program.'], ['MTA', 'Message transfer agent: mail server software using SMTP.'], ['MAA', 'Message access agent: POP3 or IMAP.'], ['Spool', 'The queue of messages waiting to be sent.'], ['Mailbox', 'Per-user file on the server where incoming mail is stored.']],
    exam: 'Define e-mail and why it needs intermediate servers, draw the architecture with UA, MTA and MAA, list the nine steps, point out the two push stages and one pull stage, and list the basic functions.',
    revise: ['UA → MTA (SMTP) → MTA (SMTP) → mailbox → MAA (POP3/IMAP) → UA', 'Push with SMTP, pull with POP3/IMAP', 'Composition, transfer, reporting, displaying, disposition'],
  },

  'cn.u5.user-agent': {
    oneLine: 'The user agent is the program people use to compose, read, reply to, forward and organise mail; it handles local mailboxes and folders, and hands outgoing mail to the mail server.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'User agent (UA)', text: 'The first component of an e-mail system: a **software package (program) that composes, reads, replies to and forwards messages**, and handles local mailboxes on the user\'s computer. It provides the interface between the user and the mail system.' },
      { h: 'Services provided by a user agent' },
      { table: { head: ['Service', 'What it does'], rows: [
        ['**Composing messages**', 'Provides a template or editor to write the message, with the header fields (To, Cc, Subject) filled in; may include spell checking, attachments and signatures.'],
        ['**Reading messages**', 'Shows a summary of the inbox (a flag for status: new, read, replied; sender; subject; size), then displays the selected message.'],
        ['**Replying to messages**', 'Sends a reply to the original sender (or to all recipients), usually quoting the original.'],
        ['**Forwarding messages**', 'Sends a received message on to a third party, with or without extra comments.'],
        ['**Handling mailboxes**', 'Maintains an **inbox** for received mail and an **outbox** for mail to be sent, plus folders the user creates to file messages.'],
      ] } },
      { h: 'Types of user agents' },
      { compare: [
        { title: 'Command-driven', items: ['Early UAs: one-letter commands at a prompt', 'e.g. r to reply, R to reply to all', 'Examples: mail, pine, elm'] },
        { title: 'GUI-based', items: ['Windows, icons, menus and mouse', 'Make using mail and attachments easy', 'Examples: Outlook, Thunderbird; web mail (Gmail) in a browser'] },
      ] },
      { h: 'Inbox summary and filtering (Tanenbaum)' },
      'When a user agent starts, it looks at the user\'s mailbox and displays a summary, one line per message, with columns such as flags (read, replied, forwarded), sender, subject and size. Users can arrange messages into **folders**, search them, and set up **filters**: rules applied to incoming mail, such as moving messages from a certain sender into a folder, or flagging likely **spam**. A **vacation agent** can automatically reply while the user is away.',
      { h: 'Sending mail from a user agent' },
      { steps: ['The user writes the message and gives the destination address (local-part@domain).', 'The UA builds the message envelope and header.', 'The UA submits it to the mail server (the user\'s MTA) using SMTP, usually on port 587 with authentication.', 'The UA does not deliver the message itself; the MTA does.'] },
      { h: 'Receiving mail' },
      'The UA uses a message access agent (**POP3** or **IMAP**) to download or view the user\'s messages from the mailbox on the mail server; web mail does this inside the server and shows it as a web page.',
    ],
    terms: [['Inbox', 'Folder holding received mail.'], ['Outbox', 'Folder holding mail waiting to be sent.'], ['Mail filter', 'A rule applied automatically to incoming messages.']],
    exam: 'Define the user agent, list and explain its five services (compose, read, reply, forward, mailbox handling), compare command-driven and GUI user agents with examples, and explain how a UA submits and receives mail.',
    revise: ['Compose, read, reply, forward, handle mailboxes', 'Command-driven vs GUI', 'UA submits to the MTA; fetches with POP3/IMAP'],
  },

  'cn.u5.formats': {
    oneLine: 'An Internet mail message (RFC 5322) is an envelope plus a message: a header of ASCII fields such as To, From, Subject and Date, a blank line, and a body; MIME extends it with headers that describe non-ASCII text, attachments and multimedia.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'Message format (RFC 5322)', text: 'The format of Internet mail messages. A message consists of a primitive **envelope** (used by the transfer agents), and the **message** itself: a number of **header fields**, a **blank line**, and the **message body**.' },
      { compare: [
        { title: 'Envelope', items: ['Used by the MTAs to deliver the message', 'Contains the destination address, priority, security level', 'Like the envelope of a postal letter'] },
        { title: 'Message (header + body)', items: ['Header: control information for the user agents', 'Body: the content, entirely for the human recipient', 'Like the letter inside the envelope'] },
      ] },
      { h: 'Header fields' },
      { table: { head: ['Header', 'Meaning'], rows: [
        ['**To:**', 'E-mail address(es) of the primary recipient(s)'],
        ['**Cc:**', 'Address(es) of secondary recipients (carbon copy)'],
        ['**Bcc:**', 'Addresses for blind carbon copies (not shown to other recipients)'],
        ['**From:**', 'Person or people who created the message'],
        ['**Sender:**', 'Address of the actual sender, if different'],
        ['**Received:**', 'Line added by each transfer agent along the route'],
        ['**Return-Path:**', 'Can be used to identify a path back to the sender'],
        ['**Date:**', 'Date and time the message was sent'],
        ['**Reply-To:**', 'Address to which replies should be sent'],
        ['**Message-Id:**', 'Unique number for referencing this message later'],
        ['**In-Reply-To:**', 'Message-Id of the message this is a reply to'],
        ['**Subject:**', 'Short summary of the message for the one-line display'],
      ] } },
      { h: 'Example message' },
      { code: `From: Alice <alice@cbit.ac.in>
To: bob@gmail.com
Cc: carol@example.com
Date: Mon, 3 Nov 2026 10:15:00 +0530
Subject: Lab timetable
Message-Id: <1234@cbit.ac.in>

Hi Bob,
The networks lab moves to Thursday this week.
Alice` },
      { h: 'MIME: Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions' },
      { def: 'MIME', text: 'A set of extensions (RFC 2045 onwards) that let Internet mail carry messages in **languages with accents or non-Latin alphabets**, and **non-text** content such as images, audio, video and binary attachments, while keeping the RFC 822/5322 format. It adds structure to the body and encoding rules for non-ASCII data.' },
      'The original format allowed only 7-bit ASCII text. MIME defines five new headers:',
      { table: { head: ['MIME header', 'Meaning'], rows: [
        ['**MIME-Version:**', 'Identifies the MIME version (1.0), telling the UA the message uses MIME'],
        ['**Content-Description:**', 'Human-readable string saying what is in the message'],
        ['**Content-Id:**', 'Unique identifier for the content part'],
        ['**Content-Transfer-Encoding:**', 'How the body is wrapped for transmission: 7bit, 8bit, binary, **base64** (groups of 24 bits as four 6-bit ASCII characters), or **quoted-printable** (mostly ASCII with = codes for the rest)'],
        ['**Content-Type:**', 'Type and subtype of the content, e.g. **text/plain**, **text/html**, **image/jpeg**, **audio/mpeg**, **video/mp4**, **application/pdf**, **multipart/mixed** (several parts, e.g. text plus attachments, separated by a boundary string), **multipart/alternative** (the same content in different formats)'],
      ] } },
      { example: 'An e-mail with a PDF attachment has Content-Type: multipart/mixed; boundary="xyz". The first part is text/plain (the message text); the second part has Content-Type: application/pdf and Content-Transfer-Encoding: base64, with the file encoded as ASCII.' },
    ],
    terms: [['Envelope', 'Delivery information used by the transfer agents.'], ['Header field', 'A line like To:, From:, Subject: describing the message.'], ['MIME', 'Extensions for non-ASCII text, attachments and multimedia in mail.'], ['base64', 'Encoding of binary data as printable ASCII, 6 bits per character.']],
    exam: 'Define the message format, distinguish envelope from message, tabulate the header fields, write a sample message, then explain MIME: why it was needed, its five headers, the content types and the transfer encodings.',
    revise: ['Envelope + header + blank line + body', 'To, Cc, Bcc, From, Date, Subject, Message-Id, Reply-To', 'MIME headers: Version, Description, Id, Transfer-Encoding, Type', 'base64 for binary; multipart/mixed for attachments'],
  },

  'cn.u5.transfer': {
    oneLine: 'Mail is transferred between mail servers by SMTP (Simple Mail Transfer Protocol): the client opens a TCP connection to port 25 and sends ASCII commands (HELO, MAIL FROM, RCPT TO, DATA, QUIT), each answered with a numeric reply code.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'SMTP (Simple Mail Transfer Protocol)', text: 'The protocol (RFC 5321) that defines the **MTA client and server** in the Internet. It is a simple **ASCII, push protocol** used twice in mail delivery: from the sender\'s user agent to its mail server, and between the sender\'s and the receiver\'s mail servers. It runs over **TCP, port 25**.' },
      { h: 'How a transfer works' },
      { steps: [
        '**Connection establishment**: the sending MTA (client) opens a TCP connection to port 25 of the receiving MTA. The server sends **220** (service ready) with its name. (If it is not prepared to accept mail, it sends 421 and the client tries later.)',
        'The client identifies itself with **HELO** (or EHLO for extended SMTP). Server: 250 OK.',
        '**Mail transfer**: the client says who the mail is from with **MAIL FROM:<address>**, then names each recipient with **RCPT TO:<address>**. The server accepts each with 250 (or rejects an unknown recipient with 550).',
        'The client sends **DATA**. The server replies **354** (start mail input) and the client sends the message: headers, a blank line and the body, ending with a line containing only a **period (.)**.',
        'The server replies 250 if it accepted the message. More messages may follow on the same connection.',
        '**Connection termination**: the client sends **QUIT**; the server replies **221** and closes the connection.',
      ] },
      { img: 'cn-smtp' },
      { h: 'SMTP commands' },
      { table: { head: ['Command', 'Meaning'], rows: [['**HELO / EHLO**', 'Client identifies itself (EHLO asks for extensions)'], ['**MAIL FROM**', 'Sender\'s address'], ['**RCPT TO**', 'A recipient\'s address (one per command)'], ['**DATA**', 'Start sending the message; it ends with a line holding a single "."'], ['**QUIT**', 'Terminate the session'], ['**RSET**', 'Abort the current mail transaction'], ['**VRFY**', 'Verify that a mailbox exists'], ['**NOOP**', 'Do nothing; check the server is alive'], ['**TURN**', 'Swap the roles of client and server']] } },
      { h: 'Reply codes' },
      { table: { head: ['Code', 'Meaning'], rows: [['**220**', 'Service ready'], ['**221**', 'Service closing the connection'], ['**250**', 'Request completed (OK)'], ['**354**', 'Start mail input'], ['**421**', 'Service not available'], ['**450**', 'Mailbox unavailable'], ['**500**', 'Syntax error, unrecognised command'], ['**550**', 'Action not taken: mailbox unavailable']] } },
      'The first digit gives the class: **2xx** success, **3xx** more input needed, **4xx** temporary failure (try again), **5xx** permanent failure.',
      { h: 'Limitations and extensions' },
      { ul: ['Original SMTP does no **authentication**: the FROM address is not checked, so spam and spoofing are easy. Mail submission from users now uses port 587 with **AUTH**.', 'It carried only 7-bit **ASCII**; binary needed base64 encoding. Extensions (**ESMTP**, requested with EHLO) add 8BITMIME, SIZE, STARTTLS (encryption) and more.', 'SMTP is a **push** protocol only: it cannot be used to pull mail from a mailbox, so the final delivery uses POP3 or IMAP.'] },
    ],
    terms: [['MTA', 'Message transfer agent, the SMTP client or server.'], ['Push protocol', 'The client pushes data to the server.'], ['Reply code', 'Three-digit SMTP status: 2xx OK, 3xx continue, 4xx temporary, 5xx permanent.'], ['ESMTP', 'Extended SMTP, negotiated with EHLO.']],
    exam: 'Define SMTP and where it is used, describe the three phases (connection, transfer, termination) with a full command/reply exchange diagram, tabulate the commands and reply codes, and list SMTP\'s limitations.',
    revise: ['TCP port 25; ASCII commands', 'HELO → MAIL FROM → RCPT TO → DATA → . → QUIT', '220 ready, 250 OK, 354 start input, 221 closing', 'Push only; no authentication in original SMTP'],
  },

  'cn.u5.delivery': {
    oneLine: 'Final delivery pulls mail from the mailbox on the server to the user: POP3 simply downloads (and usually deletes) messages, while IMAP keeps mail on the server with folders, searching and partial download.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'Final delivery (message access)', text: 'The third stage of mail delivery, from the receiver\'s mail server to the receiver\'s user agent. SMTP cannot be used, because it is a **push** protocol; final delivery needs a **pull** protocol, run by a **message access agent (MAA)**. The two protocols are **POP3** and **IMAP4**.' },
      { flow: ['Alice\'s UA', 'SMTP push', 'Alice\'s mail server', 'SMTP push', 'Bob\'s mail server (mailbox)', 'POP3 / IMAP pull', 'Bob\'s UA'] },
      { h: 'POP3: Post Office Protocol, version 3' },
      'Simple but limited. The POP3 client runs on the recipient\'s computer; the POP3 server runs on the mail server.',
      { steps: ['The client opens a TCP connection to the server on **port 110**.', '**Authorisation**: it sends the user name and password (USER, PASS).', '**Transaction**: it lists (LIST) and retrieves (RETR) messages one by one, and may delete them (DELE).', '**Update**: on QUIT, the server deletes the messages marked for deletion and closes the connection.'] },
      { compare: [
        { title: 'Delete mode', items: ['Mail is deleted from the mailbox after each retrieval', 'Used when the user works on one fixed computer', 'Mail is stored and organised locally'] },
        { title: 'Keep mode', items: ['Mail stays in the mailbox after retrieval', 'Used when the user reads mail from different computers', 'Can be read again later from elsewhere'] },
      ] },
      { h: 'IMAP4: Internet Message Access Protocol, version 4' },
      'Similar to POP3 but with more features: more powerful and more complex. It uses **port 143**. POP3 is deficient because the user cannot organise mail on the server (no folders) and cannot partially check the contents of mail before downloading. IMAP adds:',
      { ul: ['Checking the e-mail **header before downloading**.', '**Searching** the contents of e-mail for a string before downloading.', '**Partial download**: useful on limited bandwidth when a message contains large multimedia.', '**Creating, deleting or renaming mailboxes** (folders) on the server, and moving messages between them.', 'Mail stays on the server, so the user sees the **same mailbox from every device**; it also tracks flags such as read and answered.'] },
      { h: 'POP3 vs IMAP4' },
      { table: { head: ['Point', 'POP3', 'IMAP4'], rows: [['Port', '110', '143'], ['Where mail lives', 'Downloaded to the client (usually deleted from the server)', 'Kept on the server'], ['Folders on the server', 'No', 'Yes'], ['Multiple devices', 'Poor', 'Designed for it'], ['Partial download, search before download', 'No', 'Yes'], ['Complexity', 'Simple', 'Complex'], ['Server storage', 'Little', 'More']] } },
      { h: 'Web mail' },
      'With web mail (Gmail, Outlook.com) the user agent is a web page. The browser talks to the mail server using **HTTP**, not POP3 or IMAP; the server itself reads the mailbox. Sending still uses SMTP between mail servers.',
      { h: 'Other delivery features' },
      'Final delivery may also apply **filters** at the server (sorting, spam detection), **forwarding** to another address, and **vacation** auto-replies.',
    ],
    terms: [['POP3', 'Post Office Protocol: download mail from the server (port 110).'], ['IMAP4', 'Internet Message Access Protocol: manage mail on the server (port 143).'], ['Pull protocol', 'The client fetches data from the server.']],
    exam: 'Explain why final delivery needs a pull protocol, describe POP3 (port 110, the three phases, delete and keep modes), describe IMAP4 and its extra functions, and give the POP3 vs IMAP comparison table. Mention web mail over HTTP.',
    revise: ['SMTP pushes; POP3/IMAP pull', 'POP3: port 110, download, delete or keep mode', 'IMAP: port 143, server folders, search, partial download', 'Web mail uses HTTP'],
  },

  'cn.u5.ftp': {
    oneLine: 'FTP (File Transfer Protocol) copies files between hosts using two TCP connections: a control connection on port 21 that stays open for commands and replies, and a data connection on port 20 opened for each file transfer.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'FTP (File Transfer Protocol)', text: 'The standard TCP/IP protocol for **copying a file from one host to another**. It solves problems such as different file name conventions, different ways of representing text and data, and different directory structures between systems.' },
      { h: 'Two connections' },
      { img: 'cn-ftp' },
      'FTP differs from other client-server applications: it establishes **two connections** between the hosts, separating commands from data, which makes it more efficient.',
      { compare: [
        { title: 'Control connection (port 21)', items: ['Opened by the client to the server\'s port 21', 'Stays open for the **whole session**', 'Carries commands and replies, one line at a time, in NVT ASCII', 'Simple rules of communication; needs low delay'] },
        { title: 'Data connection (port 20)', items: ['Opened **for each file** transferred (or directory listing), then closed', 'Carries the file data', 'Server port 20 (active mode); in passive mode the server gives another port', 'Needs high throughput'] },
      ] },
      { h: 'FTP architecture' },
      { ul: ['The **client** has three components: the **user interface**, the **client control process** and the **client data transfer process**.', 'The **server** has two: the **server control process** and the **server data transfer process**.', 'The control processes talk over the control connection; the data transfer processes over the data connection.'] },
      { h: 'Communication over the data connection' },
      'Before transferring, the client defines three attributes over the control connection:',
      { table: { head: ['Attribute', 'Options'], rows: [['**File type**', 'ASCII file (default, text), EBCDIC file, image file (binary, sent as a continuous stream of bits)'], ['**Data structure**', 'File structure (default: a continuous stream of bytes), record structure (text files divided into records), page structure (pages with numbers and headers)'], ['**Transmission mode**', 'Stream mode (default: a continuous stream of bytes, end of file marked by closing the connection), block mode (blocks with headers), compressed mode']] } },
      { h: 'Common FTP commands' },
      { table: { head: ['Command', 'Purpose'], rows: [['USER, PASS', 'Log in with user name and password'], ['CWD, CDUP, PWD', 'Change, go up, print working directory'], ['LIST, NLST', 'List directory contents'], ['RETR filename', 'Retrieve (download) a file'], ['STOR filename', 'Store (upload) a file'], ['DELE, MKD, RMD', 'Delete a file, make or remove a directory'], ['TYPE A / TYPE I', 'Set ASCII or image (binary) type'], ['PORT / PASV', 'Choose active or passive data connection'], ['QUIT', 'End the session']] } },
      'Replies are three-digit codes, like SMTP: e.g. **220** service ready, **331** user name OK, password required, **230** user logged in, **150** opening data connection, **226** closing data connection (transfer complete), **221** goodbye.',
      { h: 'Anonymous FTP and security' },
      'Some sites offer **anonymous FTP**: the user logs in as "anonymous" with an e-mail address as the password and gets limited access to public files. FTP sends the user name and password in **plain text**, so it is insecure; **SFTP** (over SSH) or **FTPS** (over TLS) are used instead today.',
    ],
    terms: [['Control connection', 'Port 21, open for the whole session, for commands and replies.'], ['Data connection', 'Port 20 (active mode), opened per file.'], ['Anonymous FTP', 'Public access with the user name "anonymous".'], ['Passive mode', 'The client opens the data connection to a port the server names.']],
    exam: 'Define FTP, draw the two connections with ports, explain the client and server components, describe the three attributes of data transfer (file type, data structure, transmission mode), list commands and reply codes, and mention anonymous FTP and security.',
    revise: ['Control port 21 (whole session), data port 20 (per file)', 'File types: ASCII, EBCDIC, image', 'Structures: file, record, page; modes: stream, block, compressed', 'RETR download, STOR upload'],
  },

  'cn.u5.telnet': {
    oneLine: 'TELNET (TErminaL NETwork) is a general client-server remote login protocol: the user\'s keystrokes are sent over TCP port 23 to a remote computer, using a Network Virtual Terminal character set so any terminal can talk to any host.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'TELNET', text: 'Short for **TErminaL NETwork**: the standard TCP/IP protocol for **virtual terminal** service (remote login). It lets a user at one computer log in to a remote computer and use it as if the user\'s terminal were directly attached to it. It is a **general-purpose** client-server program, so one remote login service can reach any application on the remote machine.' },
      'Without remote login, a server would need a separate client-server program for every service (file transfer, mail, compiling …). TELNET lets a user log on and run any program there instead.',
      { h: 'Local login vs remote login' },
      { img: 'cn-telnet' },
      { compare: [
        { title: 'Local login', items: ['The user logs in to a time-sharing system directly', 'Keystrokes go to the terminal driver, which passes them to the operating system', 'The OS interprets them and runs the program'] },
        { title: 'Remote login', items: ['Keystrokes go to the terminal driver, then to the **TELNET client**', 'The client translates them to **NVT** characters and sends them over TCP/IP to the remote host', 'The **TELNET server** passes them to a **pseudo-terminal driver**, which hands them to the remote OS as if typed locally'] },
      ] },
      { h: 'Network Virtual Terminal (NVT)' },
      'Different computers use different character sets and different keys for the same action (end of line, interrupt). TELNET solves this heterogeneity with a universal interface, the **NVT character set**:',
      { ul: ['The client translates the local terminal\'s characters into **NVT form** and sends them; the server translates NVT form into the form the remote computer accepts. Replies are translated the other way.', '**NVT data characters** are 8-bit with the highest bit 0 (like 7-bit US ASCII).', '**NVT control characters** have the highest bit set to 1 (e.g. IAC, "interpret as control", 255, introduces a command).'] },
      { h: 'Options and option negotiation' },
      'Client and server can negotiate **options** before or during use, such as **echo** (the server echoes characters), **binary transmission**, **suppress go-ahead**, **terminal type**, **terminal speed** and **line mode**. Negotiation uses the commands **WILL, WONT, DO and DONT**: one side offers ("WILL echo") and the other accepts ("DO echo") or refuses ("DONT").',
      { h: 'Modes of operation' },
      { table: { head: ['Mode', 'How characters are sent'], rows: [['**Default mode**', 'The client echoes characters locally and sends a whole line when the user presses Enter'], ['**Character mode**', 'Each character is sent to the server as typed; the server echoes it back (more traffic, slower over long links)'], ['**Line mode**', 'Line editing (echo, erase) is done by the client, which sends whole lines']] } },
      { h: 'User interface and security' },
      'Common user commands: open (connect to a host), close, display, mode, set, status, send, quit. TELNET is used today mainly for **diagnostics** (e.g. connecting to a mail server on port 25 to test it). It sends everything, including passwords, **in plain text**, so for remote login it has been replaced by **SSH** (Secure Shell, port 22), which encrypts the session.',
    ],
    terms: [['Remote login', 'Using a remote computer as though your terminal were attached to it.'], ['NVT', 'Network Virtual Terminal: the common character set used by TELNET.'], ['Pseudo-terminal driver', 'Server-side software that feeds remote keystrokes to the OS as if from a terminal.'], ['SSH', 'Secure Shell: encrypted replacement for TELNET (port 22).']],
    exam: 'Define TELNET, compare local and remote login with a diagram, explain the NVT and why it is needed, describe option negotiation (WILL, WONT, DO, DONT) and the three modes, and note its security weakness and SSH.',
    revise: ['Remote login over TCP port 23', 'Client → NVT over the Internet → server → pseudo-terminal → OS', 'Options negotiated with WILL/WONT/DO/DONT', 'Plain text; replaced by SSH'],
  },

  'cn.u5.snmp': {
    oneLine: 'SNMP (Simple Network Management Protocol) lets a manager station monitor and control agents on routers and hosts: the agent keeps variables in a MIB, named and typed using SMI, and the manager reads and sets them with Get and Set requests while agents send Traps for unusual events.',
    blocks: [
      { def: 'SNMP', text: 'The **Simple Network Management Protocol**: a framework for **managing devices in an internet using the TCP/IP suite**. It provides a set of fundamental operations for **monitoring and maintaining** an internet, based on a **manager** and **agents**. It is an application-level protocol, so it can manage devices made by different manufacturers on different physical networks.' },
      { img: 'cn-snmp' },
      { h: 'Managers and agents' },
      { ul: ['A **manager** is a host that runs the **SNMP client** program (the management station).', 'An **agent** is a router or host that runs the **SNMP server** program (the managed station).', 'The agent keeps performance information in a **database**; the manager has access to the values in it.'] },
      'Management with SNMP is based on three basic ideas:',
      { ol: ['A manager **checks** an agent by requesting information that reflects its behaviour (e.g. a router\'s packets received and forwarded, to see if it is congested).', 'A manager **forces** an agent to perform a task by resetting values in its database (e.g. setting a reboot counter to 0 to reboot it remotely).', 'An agent **contributes** to management by warning the manager of an unusual situation with a **Trap** message.'] },
      { h: 'Management components' },
      'SNMP does not work alone; it uses two other protocols:',
      { table: { head: ['Component', 'Role'], rows: [
        ['**SMI** (Structure of Management Information)', 'Defines the general rules for **naming objects**, defining **object types** (integer, IP address, counter, gauge, string …) and **encoding** objects and values (Basic Encoding Rules). Objects are named with a hierarchical **object identifier** (OID) such as 1.3.6.1.2.1 (iso.org.dod.internet.mgmt.mib-2).'],
        ['**MIB** (Management Information Base)', 'Creates, for each entity to be managed, the **set of objects** (variables) it must have, grouped into categories such as system, interface, address translation, ip, icmp, tcp, udp, egp, transmission, snmp.'],
        ['**SNMP**', 'Defines the **format of the packets** exchanged between manager and agent, and how they read and change object values.'],
      ] } },
      { analogy: 'Like writing a program: SMI is the language\'s rules for declaring variables and types, the MIB is the set of variables declared for each device, and SNMP is the statements that read and write those variables.' },
      { h: 'SNMP PDUs (message types)' },
      { table: { head: ['PDU', 'Sent by', 'Purpose'], rows: [['**GetRequest**', 'Manager', 'Retrieve the value of one or more variables'], ['**GetNextRequest**', 'Manager', 'Retrieve the value of the next variable (walk a table)'], ['**GetBulkRequest**', 'Manager', 'Retrieve a large amount of data, e.g. a whole table'], ['**SetRequest**', 'Manager', 'Set (store) a value in a variable'], ['**Response**', 'Agent', 'Reply to a Get or Set request with the values or an error'], ['**Trap**', 'Agent', 'Report an event, e.g. a reboot or link failure, without being asked'], ['**InformRequest**', 'Manager', 'Send information to another (remote) manager; must be acknowledged'], ['**Report**', 'Manager', 'Report some types of errors between managers']] } },
      { h: 'Transport' },
      'SNMP uses **UDP**: the agent listens on **port 161** for requests, and the manager listens on **port 162** for Traps. UDP keeps the protocol light; requests that are lost can simply be repeated. SNMPv3 adds **security** (authentication and privacy), which earlier versions lacked.',
    ],
    terms: [['Manager', 'The station running the SNMP client that monitors devices.'], ['Agent', 'The SNMP server on a managed router or host.'], ['MIB', 'The set of managed objects for a device.'], ['SMI', 'Rules for naming, typing and encoding managed objects.'], ['Trap', 'An unsolicited alert from an agent to the manager.']],
    exam: 'Define SNMP, draw the manager–agent picture, explain the three basic ideas, describe the roles of SMI, MIB and SNMP (with the programming analogy), tabulate the PDUs, and state the UDP ports 161 and 162.',
    revise: ['Manager (client) controls agents (servers)', 'SMI: names and types; MIB: the objects; SNMP: the messages', 'GetRequest, GetNext, GetBulk, Set, Response, Trap, Inform, Report', 'UDP 161 (agent), 162 (traps)'],
  },
}
